  "narrative": "No new validation deadlines are open, so this synthesis consolidates the current evidence and allocates the next analyses. I use the supplied dataset-level framework: a signed lower bound above +0.10 is treated as evidence for a clinically meaningful association in the registered direction; a signed upper bound below +0.10 or an opposite-direction effect is treated as unsupported; intervals crossing +0.10 are treated as unresolved uncertainty rather than contradictions of the central result. All conclusions are associations within this lung-lineage CRISPR dataset and are not causal mechanisms or externally generalizable claims.\n\nThe five independently validated central associations are stable and large enough to support strong dataset-level conclusions. First, MSI-high is associated with much stronger WRN dependency: discovery H6 R6 signed effect +0.790 with 95% interval +0.760 to +0.819, and independent validation R52 +0.802 with strict interval +0.753 to +0.851. Because the claim direction is -1 on the dependency-score scale, this means a raw shift toward more negative WRN dependency scores, i.e. stronger dependency. This effect is far above the +0.10 benchmark and is robust across many tested technical and biological contexts, including libraries, batches, TP53 contexts, lineage subtype, STK11/CDKN2A contexts, and matched multimodal QC.\n\nSecond, SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency: discovery H8 R8 signed effect +0.784, interval +0.765 to +0.803; independent validation R13 +0.792, strict interval +0.763 to +0.822. This is a raw shift toward more negative SMARCA2 dependency scores. The effect is large and broadly consistent across multiple screened contexts, including batch, library, TP53, STK11, CDKN2A, and KY contexts.\n\nThird, KEAP1 loss is associated with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, not stronger: the accepted reversal H20 has signed effect +0.796, interval +0.776 to +0.816, with independent validation R39 +0.806, strict interval +0.775 to +0.838. This corresponds to a raw shift toward less negative, i.e. weaker, NFE2L2 dependency scores. This is one of the most robust effects in the dataset across many subgroups and screen contexts.\n\nFourth, TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency: H15 R17 signed +0.396, interval +0.325 to +0.467; validation R26 +0.395, strict interval +0.293 to +0.498. This is a raw shift toward less negative USP7 dependency scores, i.e. weaker dependency. The central effect is robust across Avana library, batch, matched QC, CDKN2A wild-type, MSI-negative, STK11-related, and MTAP-loss contexts. The KY-library context H80 is directionally supportive and was accepted only as borderline context evidence: discovery R73 lower bound +0.051, validation R91 strict lower bound +0.106 with wide uncertainty. This does not overturn the central TP53/USP7 association but should be reported as a less precise technical context.\n\nFifth, PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency: H93 discovery R88 signed +0.267, interval +0.215 to +0.319; independent validation R90 +0.329, strict interval +0.255 to +0.402. Because the claim direction is -1, this is a raw shift toward more negative STAT3 dependency scores. The primary claim is independently supported. Robustness checks are now broadly consistent: batch_a H95 lower +0.125, adenocarcinoma H97 lower +0.206, matched RNA/DNA/CRISPR-QC H99 lower +0.221, PIK3CA-mutant H100 lower +0.127 with small exposed-cell caution, PIK3CA-wild-type H101 lower +0.210, and TP53-intact H103 lower +0.227. Two secondary contexts remain unresolved rather than contradictory: KY library H96 signed +0.158 but interval +0.063 to +0.254, and TP53-loss co-mutation context H102 signed +0.190, interval +0.085 to +0.296. These suggest context-specific precision loss, not evidence against the overall validated PTEN/STAT3 association.\n\nThe negative evidence is also informative. Many biologically motivated direct-target or pathway hypotheses were rejected because their signed intervals either crossed zero or remained well below the +0.10 benchmark, or moved in the opposite direction. These include KRAS mutation as a predictor of KRAS dependency, BRAF V600E as a predictor of BRAF dependency, EGFR amplification as a predictor of EGFR dependency, ERBB2 amplification as a predictor of ERBB2 dependency, MYC amplification against KIF18A, POLR2A, RPL11, DHX9, and TMED10, BRCA2 loss against WRN, DHX9, and POLR2A, ARID1A loss against SMARCA2, STK11 loss against TMED10 and NFE2L2 contexts, MTAP loss against USP7 or MDM2 in most contexts, PIK3CA mutation against STAT3, RB1 loss against KIF18A, and the constructed research markers D, E, and F across WRN, SMARCA2, USP7, NFE2L2, and STAT3. These are best described as unsupported dataset-level hypotheses within the tested comparisons, not proof of absence.\n\nThe next most informative work is to resolve whether the new PTEN/STAT3 association is modified by major co-mutation architecture, especially KRAS and TP53 context. The already active subgroup means H107 and H108 are valuable, and I have now registered an interaction of PTEN-loss/STAT3 dependency by KRAS mutation and another by TP53 loss to ask the sharper effect-modification question. I also registered a TP53-loss/USP7 interaction by KEAP1 loss to test whether a central oxidative-stress co-mutation context changes the otherwise robust TP53/USP7 association. The existing active RB1-loss POLR2A probe H109 remains useful as a final targeted discovery screen. If these analyses produce a new robust effect with lower bound above +0.10, it should be accepted as a dataset-level association and considered for independent validation; if they produce wide crossing intervals, they should be reported as unresolved; if they produce nulls, they should be closed as unsupported. No validation request is warranted now because the central associations have already been independently validated and the remaining questions are either robustness or exploratory effect modification.",
  "research_notes": "Current supported central dataset-level associations, all independently validated: (1) MSI-high -> much stronger WRN dependency, raw more-negative shift about -0.79 to -0.80, very robust. (2) SMARCA4 loss -> much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, raw more-negative shift about -0.78 to -0.79, very robust. (3) KEAP1 loss -> much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, raw less-negative/positive shift about +0.80, very robust. (4) TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency, raw less-negative/positive shift about +0.395, robust overall; KY context H80 accepted only as borderline supportive. (5) PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3 dependency, raw more-negative shift about -0.267 discovery and -0.329 validation. PTEN/STAT3 robustness accepted in batch_a, adenocarcinoma, matched RNA/DNA/CRISPR-QC, PIK3CA-wild-type, PIK3CA-mutant small-cell caution, and TP53-intact. Deferred/borderline PTEN/STAT3 contexts: KY library H96 lower 0.063 and TP53-loss co-mutation H102 lower 0.085; PIK3CA interaction H104 near zero but wide. TP53/USP7 accepted in matched QC H105. Negative/rejected: many direct target dependencies, MYC amplification probes, BRCA2 probes, ARID1A/SMARCA2, MTAP/MDM2/USP7, PIK3CA/STAT3, RB1/KIF18A, research markers. Deferred unresolved claims without results are not negative evidence. Next priorities: H107-H108 active PTEN/STAT3 KRAS strata, newly registered H110 PTEN/STAT3 interaction by KRAS, H111 PTEN/STAT3 interaction by TP53, H112 TP53/USP7 interaction by KEAP1, and H109 RB1/POLR2A discovery. Use +0.10 lower-bound benchmark for acceptance; upper below +0.10 rejects in registered direction; crossing +0.10 means unresolved. All are associations in supplied lung-lineage CRISPR dataset, not causal or generalizable claims."
}

